me.\n\n# Mathematical Specification\nLet `x` be our sequence of length `n` (we will vary `n`). The objective function is:\n`f(x) = 2*n*max_{k} (convolve(x, x)[k]) / (sum(x))**2`\n\nBut note: we must keep `sum(x)` at least 0.01. Hence, we can define `x` by a normalized version: let `s = sum(x)` and `x_normalized = x/s`.\n\nAlternatively, we work with `a` and `g` (dual direction sequences) and leverage the linearity of the convolution.\n\nThe optimization problem is to find:\n`min_x f(x)`\nsubject to `x >=0`.\n\nWe define the gradient operator `\u2207f` to project updates onto the simplex while also using smoothed peak information. Specifically:\n- The gradient is derived from the auto-correlation: `convolve(x, x)`\n- We then use the max norm to define a metric for descent direction.\n\nThe constraints are the non-negativity, and our search must produce a sequence output that the original `evaluate_sequence` function can handle. All iterations are normalized to have `sum(x)=1` to speed up evaluation.\n\n# Implementation Procedure\n\nStep 1: Define the `peak_coordinate_descent` function as given in the previous program, but with an added gradient smoothing step. We focus on reducing the auto-correlation peaks by reallocating mass in the most efficient manner.\n\nStep 2: Choose several candidate lengths and execute the optimization process for each, within a time budget. We then pick the best one (lowest objective) found.\n\nStep 3: For each chosen length, implement a coordinate descent algorithm that:\n   - Normalizes the sequence.\n   - Computes auto-correlation.\n   - Identifies the top `K` convolution peaks.\n   - Uses gradient information to determine where to move mass away from (to reduce peaks).\n\nStep 4: Add a line search method to find the optimal learning rate along each projection step.\n\nStep 5: Combine with simulated annealing and temperature control to escape shallow local minima.\n\nStep 6: Use Nesterov-style momentum when possible.\n\nThe key mathematical changes are:\n- We compute the gradient by considering the discrete indices of the convolution.\n- We smooth the gradient by only considering a subset (top K) of the convolution values to avoid numerical noise.\n\n## Implementation Details:\nWe define a helper function `project_simplex` that performs the Euclidean projection to the simplex:\n\n```python\ndef project_simplex(v):\n    n_len = len(v)\n    sorted_v = np.sort(v)[::-1]\n    cssv = np.cumsum(sorted_v)\n    helper_arr = sorted_v * np.arange(1, n_len + 1) > (cssv - 1)\n    if not np.any(helper_arr):\n        return np.ones(n_len) / n_len\n    rho = (helper_arr).nonzero()\n    rho = rho[0][-1]\n    theta = (cssv[rho] - 1.0) / (rho + 1.0)\n    return np.maximum(v - theta, 0.0)\n```\n\nThis function ensures that `v` is normalized to the simplex and is non-negative.\n\nFor the main optimization, we implement a coordinate descent method that mimics the previous program but with gradients applied to the max positions. We also incorporate a line search mechanism to adaptively choose the step size:\n\n```python\ndef peak_coordinate_descent(a_old, time_limit):\n    a = np.maximum(a_old.astype(np.float64), 0.0)\n    s_old = a.sum()\n    if s_old < 1e-15:\n        # if the sum is too small, use uniform\n        a = np.ones(len(a)) / len(a)\n        s_old = a.sum()\n    # Normalize to unit L1\n    a = a / s_old\n\n    best_score = float('inf')\n    best_a = a.copy()\n\n    def evaluate(a_current):\n        try:\n            score_val = evaluate_sequence(a_current)\n            return score_val\n        except:\n            return float('inf')\n\n    conv = fast_convolve(a)\n    max_conv = conv.max()\n\n    # Check if current score is valid\n    if best_score > max_conv:\n        best_score = max_conv\n        best_a = a.copy()\n\n    # Setup gradient calculation\n    # Gradient: try to move mass from indices that contribute most to high conv\n    def compute_gradient(a_current):\n        conv = fast_convolve(a_curr